WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.549
Netflix has been ramping up its programming
slate in a big way in recent years, for better

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:05.549
and for worse.

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:08.980
There have been hit series that make it a
must-subscribe like Orange Is the New Black

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:14.150
and Stranger Things, but there have also been
a lot of projects that were severely over-hyped.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.920
No entertainment giant is perfect, and with
that in mind, here's a look at some Netflix

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.930
originals that didn't live up to the hype.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.990
Marco Polo

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.800
Marco Polo was hyped by Netflix as, quote,
"a timeless tale of power, adventure, betrayal

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.930
and lust that combines deft storytelling and
cinematic ambition."

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.699
Despite all that lofty language, however,
the series was a bonafide flop.

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:39.149
Sure, the show did earn some points for boasting
a culturally appropriate cast, and the company's

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:44.179
CEO lauded its popularity and called it a
"massive success,” though the 24% Rotten

00:00:44.179 --> 00:00:48.350
Tomatoes score for the show's first season
suggests critics thought otherwise.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.039
After two seasons, Netflix couldn't continue
funding the series and cancelled it, with

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:55.730
a reported $200 million loss to show for all
their efforts.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.089
"Why do we need more?

00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:02.299
I will tell you: because that is what comes
next."

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:03.299
Sense8

00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:07.509
When the Wachowskis set out to make their
first small-screen series, their names alone

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:11.000
were enough to draw some enthusiastic interest
in the sci-fi show.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.290
It certainly didn't hurt that Sense8 was met
with mostly positive reviews and was recognized

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.920
by the GLAAD Media Awards for its inclusion
of LGBTQ characters.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.820
After two seasons, however, Netflix decided
to pull the plug on the series.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.790
The show simply didn't attract enough of a
following to justify its cost of about $9

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.670
million an episode.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.409
Following a roaring audience reaction to the
show's cancellation, Netflix did decide to

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:37.619
bring it back for a single two-hour finale
episode to offer closure.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:38.619
The Get Down

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:45.050
This '70s-set musical-drama cost a whopping
$120 million to make, making it Netflix's

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.920
priciest series up to that point.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.700
The streaming service also pulled out all
the stops to promote The Get Down, including

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.770
using graffiti art in big cities.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.759
The investment appeared sound, and favorable
reviews certainly seemed to echo that.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.240
However, the show's viewership didn't quite
measure up to expectations, with just over

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.659
three million pairs of eyes tuning in during
the first month of its run.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:08.670
The show's failure to match the ratings of
far less expensive original series meant it

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.300
was predictably canned after a single two-part
season.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.100
"You made it this far.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:18.080
Just keep loving me and I promise it's gonna
all be alright."

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.670
Marvel's Iron Fist

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.610
Netflix had been on a roll with its Marvel
adaptations, but Iron Fist was met with stunning

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:29.020
disfavor, despite being heavily binged upon
release after a bevy of pre-release hype comparing

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.610
the series to its well-liked predecessors.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.760
Some critics declared the inaugural season
a directionless snoozefest, while others took

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.230
umbrage with the appeal of the title hero
himself.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.700
It didn't help that the show was also beset
with allegations of cultural appropriation.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.180
Iron Fist's critical whomping didn't spark
a downward trend for the streaming service's

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.370
Marvel shows to come, but season one showrunner
Scott Buck was still replaced for the eventual

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.370
second season.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.760
"Let’s go, you don’t belong here."

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.990
"I’m Danny Rand!"

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:55.990
Disjointed

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:59.450
It isn't just punnery to say that Netflix
had high hopes for Disjointed.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.460
The pot-centric series featured Kathy Bates
as a cannabis shop owner and was spearheaded

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.630
by TV sitcom giant Chuck Lorre.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:10.340
In other words, it was a stoner show with
solid talent in front of and behind the cameras.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.620
What could possibly go wrong?

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:15.690
Well, despite some stylish marketing efforts
that included branded marijuana strains being

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.200
doled out at a pop-up dispensary, the series
simply failed to resonate with its intended

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.200
audience.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.660
Critics found it to be plagued by obvious
jokes and a scattered plot that unfortunately

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.370
made good on the series' name, and the show
ended up being the worst-reviewed series of

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.370
the summer.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.470
After a two-part season, the show was unceremoniously
snuffed out by Netflix.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.591
"I am cool, because I don't take pot.

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:39.260
Pot is lame."

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.070
(crowd gasps)

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.070
Bright

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:48.180
This late 2017 sci-fi/fantasy movie was the
focus of a massive bidding war that resulted

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.620
in Netflix forking over $90 million for the
rights.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.090
Bright featured Will Smith and Joel Edgerton
as cross-species buddy cop leads, specifically,

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:01.130
a human and an orc, both serving as an extended
metaphor for real-life racial tensions.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:04.760
Despite an aggressive front-page promotional
push by Netflix, the reviews for the film

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.490
were an absolute bloodbath.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:11.110
Even so, Netflix wasn't deterred by the bad
buzz and decided to greenlight a sequel.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.910
"Now you keep doing all your gangster stuff.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:15.800
I'm just trying to sell my house."

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.410
The Cloverfield Paradox

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:21.870
With the third installment in J.J. Abrams'
loosely connected series of Cloverfield films,

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.870
it was the method of delivery that felt like
a big power play: Netflix dropped the film's

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:30.470
trailer during the 2018 Super Bowl and revealed
that it'd be available for streaming just

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:31.630
after the game was over.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:35.990
Thanks to the unexpected success of the first
sequel, 10 Cloverfield Lane, and the depth

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.290
of talent in Paradox’s cast, the film was
watched by five million people during its

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.090
first week online.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.250
Despite that savvy marketing, though, the
film was absolutely eviscerated by critics,

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:49.980
who were even more disappointed in the film
after its major marketing splash.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:56.200
(basically everyone's thoughts on the Cloverfield
series after this movie)

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:58.400
Mute

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.010
Duncan Jones waited a long time to make Mute,
the so-called "spiritual sequel" to his stunning

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.260
2009 sci-fi film Moon.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:10.540
When Netflix finally helped usher the 16-years-in-the-making
movie into reality, Jones fans were definitely

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:11.630
paying attention.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:15.540
Perhaps Jones should've let the film percolate
a few more years, however, because the final

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.950
product was lackluster, to put it kindly.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.000
After all that build-up, what resulted was
an ultimately lifeless, disorganized, and

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.840
derivative film that didn't come anywhere
near living up to the expectations set by

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:26.840
its creator.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.000
“It’s hard to be this good looking.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.380
Big responsibility."

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.140
"Oh, yeah, that’s it.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.140
Thanks for watching!

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.400
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.400
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

